    {
      "title": "Conceptual VRAM Checklist for 12GB GPU LLM Deployment (Hypothetical)",
      "body": "Considering a hypothetical scenario for running local LLMs on a 12GB GPU, this checklist aims to provide a structured approach to VRAM planning. It's important to stress that this framework is entirely theoretical, and no specific models or runtimes were evaluated. The core idea is to categorize VRAM usage into distinct allocations: model weights, the KV cache, and miscellaneous runtime needs.\n\nFor a 12GB GPU, managing these components is key. The model weights themselves will consume a baseline amount, which can be reduced via quantization. The KV cache is dynamic and grows with sequence length and batch size, often requiring careful consideration to avoid out-of-memory errors. Other runtime allocations include the inference engine's memory footprint, CUDA context, and any temporary buffers. A prudent hypothetical allocation might reserve ~50-60% for weights, ~20-30% for the KV cache, and the remainder for overheads, but this is highly speculative.\n\nThis is a purely conceptual exercise to guide thinking about resource constraints. Real-world performance and VRAM usage will differ significantly based on the exact model, quantization, and inference settings. What are others' theoretical approaches to VRAM budgeting under similar constraints?",
